WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.160
[Auto-generated transcript. Edits may have been applied for clarity.]
Telling the difference between structural chords and embellishing chords can be an interesting new concept for a lot of people.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:13.920
And I want to use a tune — "Real Emotional Girl" by Randy Newman — to walk you through this, because I think it's a beautiful song,

3
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.800
I really enjoy it, and it just matches this topic really well.

4
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.120
If you haven't yet,

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.400
go back and listen to it with the sheet music that I've provided and come right back to this video and I'll walk you through it.

6
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.280
So it starts with these two chords, which is great.

7
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.680
We can already see from the beginning that we have a G chord moving to a G7 chord,

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.600
and it changes inversion right as it does that: root position, first inversion seventh chord...

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.840
and that's [ARPEGGIATION CHORD] one of our embellishing chord types. So we have a structural "pillar," sort of,

10
00:00:50.640 --> 00:01:01.000
and then a second chord that simply changes that in a way that moves the bass note to a half step below where we're going.

11
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.110
So, embellishing chords have the power to move — especially in the middle of a bar — from one chord to another.

12
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:17.750
And there's always an approach to the embellishing chord and then moving away from it, a departure.

13
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:24.790
And so in this case, the approach is an arpeggiation, and the departure is a step up.

14
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:39.390
That step-up chord moves right to a C/E (so a C chord over a E bass note). Again, our C chord arpeggiates and then moves up by step to the F chord.

15
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.510
And then we have this beautiful dissonance in that melody of the piano there.

16
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:54.150
But as that resolves, our bass note shifts too, from root to first inversion.

17
00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:04.790
And then we have the opposite. On the next bar, we have an unstable inverted chord that progresses to a root position G,

18
00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:13.750
which moves through regular root position motion from the dominant chord to the tonic chord up to G.

19
00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:23.740
So we have our first phrase already in place, the introduction moving to a tonic chord structural chord.

20
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.780
But it's inversion changes to make an embellishing chord.

21
00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.940
Moving to another structural chord, it inverts to have an embellishing chord.

22
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:42.180
We start this one with an embellishing chord that moves to a root position structural one,

23
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:48.740
and then we have a set of three chords in a row C: G/B, Am.

24
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.300
So that's a new embellishing chords type: a PASSING CHORD.

25
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.980
We're going to use a lot of the same terminology here that we use for non-chord tones,

26
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:05.500
A passing chord — like a passing tone — instead of having a passing tone

27
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:12.260
(that would be a dissonance), it's like a passing tone that's been harmonized with a dominant —

28
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:24.170
so, a "passing chord." And then this is a second inversion chord moving to a root position.

29
00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:31.050
So that's another kind of arpeggiation. We have some dissonances playing out here with the Am.

30
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:41.130
Another passing chord (C/G). A lot of times those second inversion chords work as passing chords.

31
00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:48.530
We get to this F that moves to an E7.

32
00:03:52.810 --> 00:04:00.570
Now this is interesting: it is a dominant chord. It's a little unstable because it's in first inversion, but it doesn't move to its root position.

33
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:08.690
It simply resolves like we might expect it to up by a step.

34
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:13.410
Except in this case it is shifted for emotional effect.

35
00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:18.210
Ooh, a "real emotional" effect (!) up to the other octave.

36
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:28.570
And when we get to this, a minor chord in bar 10, we have a structural chord of Am and then a series of passing chords: Am/G...

37
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:43.560
...all of these inverted... D7/ F#,  halfdim7/F, and then finally we move up again to this C/G,

38
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:53.080
which is unstable, and it changes to another unstable chord finally to a structural [chord] - that F.

39
00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.960
And then we have a C/G, (another inverted chord that's going to be a PEDAL CHORD).

40
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.680
G act as the pedal tone, but it's harmonized with a C chord, and then it resolves.

41
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:17.520
And then we have some tonicization chords that move to the structural F.

42
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:26.240
C/G inverted: that arpeggiating bass again,

43
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.680
finally resolving to this beautiful subdominant cadence.
